course: biology
lesson: 396
title: Cartoon animals put right: Scooby-Doo, Garfield, Sonic, Winnie the Pooh and Woody Woodpecker
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Scooby-Doo shakes with fear, Garfield wolfs a tray of lasagne, Sonic zooms round loops faster than sound. Cartoons are fun because they bend the truth, but behind each one is a real animal with its own amazing story.
explain: A real Great Dane is huge but gentle and calm. A real cat cannot even taste sugar. A real hedgehog is brown, trots at about walking speed and rolls into a ball of spines to protect itself, not to attack.
explain: Some cartoons get things right: cats really do sleep for most of the day, and woodpeckers really do hammer wood again and again with heads built to take the knocks.
explain: Winnie the Pooh was even named partly after a real bear, a gentle black bear called Winnipeg who lived at London Zoo. So enjoy the cartoon, then meet the real animal.
fact: Scooby-Doo is a Great Dane, one of the tallest dog breeds; real Great Danes are usually gentle and calm, and their size came from being bred long ago to hunt big wild boar.
fact: A big Great Dane standing on its back legs can be taller than a grown man, and big dogs often live shorter lives than small dogs.
fact: Dogs gulp food because their wild relatives had to eat fast before others stole it; many human foods like chocolate and onions are bad for dogs, and a Great Dane's stomach can twist if it eats too much too fast, which is an emergency for a vet.
fact: Real cats are meat-eaters and cannot taste sweet things at all, and rich human food like lasagne makes them fat and ill; but cats really do sleep a lot, often 12 to 16 hours a day.
fact: About four out of five orange cats are male, because orange fur is passed down from parents to kittens in a way that works differently for boys and girls.
fact: Real hedgehogs are brown, not blue, and trot at about a person's walking speed; their spines are stiff hairs that protect them when they roll into a ball, which they do to defend, not to attack.
fact: An adult hedgehog has about 5,000 to 7,000 spines, each lasting about a year before a new one replaces it; baby hedgehogs are born with soft spines under their skin so the birth does not hurt the mother.
fact: Winnie the Pooh was named partly after Winnipeg, a gentle American black bear cub taken from Canada by a soldier and later given to London Zoo, where children were allowed to visit her, and partly after a swan.
fact: Piglets, owls, rabbits and donkeys could live in an English wood, but kangaroos come from Australia and tigers from Asia; a real tiger uses its tail for balance, while kangaroos really do use their tails as a fifth leg when moving slowly.
fact: Real woodpeckers drum on wood to find insects, make nest holes and call to other woodpeckers; their skulls, neck muscles and beaks are built to take hard knocks, and some peck about 20 times a second.
fact: Most woodpeckers have two toes forward and two backward, like a climbing hook, and lean back on stiff tail feathers on a tree trunk; their tongue is so long it wraps round inside the skull.
exercise: What breed of dog is Scooby-Doo? | A Great Dane | A Labrador | A poodle | A German shepherd
exercise: What are real Great Danes usually like? | Gentle and calm | Scared of everything | Small and noisy | Fierce and wild
exercise: What were Great Danes bred to hunt long ago? | Big wild boar | Mice and rats | Ducks in ponds | Fish in rivers
exercise: Why do dogs gulp their food? | Their wild relatives had to eat fast before others stole it | They cannot taste anything at all, so they do not bother to chew it slowly | Their teeth are too blunt to chew | They are always starving
exercise: Which human food is bad for dogs? | Chocolate | Plain cooked chicken | Plain water | Dog biscuits
exercise: Which taste can a real cat not taste at all? | Sweet | Salty | Sour | Bitter
exercise: What does Garfield get right about real cats? | Cats sleep a lot, often 12 to 16 hours a day | Cats love lasagne more than any other food | Cats really do hate Mondays | Cats can taste sugar
exercise: About how many orange cats are male? | About four out of five | About one in ten | Exactly half of them | None at all
exercise: What colour are real hedgehogs? | Brown | Blue | Green | Bright red
exercise: Why does a hedgehog roll into a ball? | To protect itself with its spines | To spin round fast and attack its enemies | To roll down hills for fun | To keep its spines warm
exercise: About how many spines does an adult hedgehog have? | About 5,000 to 7,000 | About 50 | About a million | About 500
exercise: Who was Winnipeg? | A gentle black bear at London Zoo | A brown bear who did tricks in a travelling circus | A soldier in Canada | A white swan in a park
exercise: Which of Pooh's friends could not live wild in an English wood? | The kangaroo | The brown owl | The wild rabbit | The grey donkey
exercise: What does a real tiger use its tail for? | Balance when running and climbing | Bouncing up and down like a spring | Holding on to its food | Swatting snakes away
exercise: Why do real woodpeckers drum on wood? | To find insects, make nest holes and call to others | To sharpen their beaks so they are ready for fighting other birds | To make people laugh | To keep their heads warm in winter
exercise: How are most woodpeckers' toes arranged? | Two forward and two backward | All four of them pointing forward | Three forward and none back | Five forward and one back
exercise: What helps a woodpecker lean back on a tree trunk? | Its stiff tail feathers | Its long tongue | Its sharp teeth | Its sticky feet
test: «Scooby's a Great Dane so they're all scaredy-cats» What is the best correction? | Real Great Danes are usually gentle and calm, not scaredy-cats | Right, Great Danes are scared of everything, even their own shadows | Great Danes are tiny lapdogs | Great Danes only live in cartoons
test: Scooby and Shaggy share a giant sandwich in one gulp. What could happen to a real Great Dane that eats too much too fast? | Its stomach can twist, which is an emergency for a vet | It would grow even taller and stronger than other Great Danes | Nothing, because big dogs can eat anything at all | It would start talking like Scooby
test: Why do some dog bowls have bumps inside? | To slow a dog down so it does not gulp | To clean the dog's teeth while it is eating | To make the food taste better | To stop the bowl sliding on the floor
test: «gonna give my cat a slice of cake for her birthday, she'll love the sugar» What is true? | Cats cannot taste sweet, and rich human food is bad for them | Cats love sugar even more than meat, so a slice of cake is a treat | Cake is the best food for cats | Cats only eat cake on birthdays
test: Count them: Sonic and his friend Shadow are real hedgehogs with about 6,000 spines each. How many spines together, roughly? | About 12,000 | About 6,000 | About 60,000 | About 1,200
test: In the game, Sonic curls into a ball and smashes into robots. What does a real hedgehog do when it rolls up? | It defends itself and waits for danger to pass | It charges straight at its enemies and knocks them over | It bounces away like a ball | It shoots out its spines like darts
test: «hedgehogs are speedy, that's why Sonic is one» What is the real speed of a hedgehog? | It trots at about a person's walking speed | It runs faster than a car on a motorway | It is faster than a cheetah | It never moves at all
test: Why would a zoo today never let visiting children play with a bear, as children did with Winnipeg? | Bears are strong wild animals and can hurt people, even if usually calm | Bears today are all asleep for the whole year and cannot be woken up to play | Children are not allowed in zoos | Bears are now smaller than children
test: Count them: Pooh, Piglet, Eeyore and Tigger stand in a row as real animals. How many tails? | 4 | 3 | 8 | 1
test: Tigger bounces about on his tail. Which real animal really leans on its tail, like a fifth leg? | The kangaroo | The tiger | The donkey | The owl
test: Count them: a woodpecker has 2 feet with 4 toes each. How many toes point backwards? | 4 | 2 | 8 | 0
test: What would happen to a real woodpecker's head when it pecks 20 times a second? | Its skull, neck muscles and beak are built to take the knocks | It would get a bad headache and have to stop pecking for a few days | It would break its beak each time | It would fall off the tree
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the temperament of the Great Dane? | It is ordinarily gentle and calm, notwithstanding its considerable size | It is invariably timid and fearful, precisely as depicted in animated films | It is a small and excitable breed | It is incapable of domestication
exam: Identify the taste which the domestic cat is incapable of perceiving. | Sweetness | Saltiness | Sourness | Bitterness
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the defensive behaviour of the hedgehog. | It rolls itself into a ball so that its spines protect it from attack | It rolls itself into a ball in order to assault its enemies at considerable speed | It discharges its spines towards its enemies | It escapes by running faster than any predator
exam: It is given that most woodpeckers possess two toes directed forward and two directed backward on each foot. Ascertain the total number of toes directed forward upon both feet. | Four | Two | Eight | Six
